WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.410
Imagine two sensors watching the same machine.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.993
The fault has not changed, but their signals look different.

00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:11.130
Now change the speed or temperature, and the signals change again.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:17.677
My question is: what should a diagnostic model preserve, when the way we observe a fault keeps changing?

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:21.892
I think of the representation in two parts.

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:26.968
A fault-semantic core carries information useful for identifying the fault.

00:00:27.158 --> 00:00:31.465
A complementary residual retains other aspects of the response.

00:00:31.655 --> 00:00:35.147
The aim is not to make every sensor feature identical.

00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:40.310
It is to preserve diagnostic meaning while allowing the observations to differ.

00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:45.190
The first design choice is to use operating context.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.273
Temperature helps describe the state in which a signal was measured.

00:00:49.463 --> 00:00:53.654
The model uses that context when organizing the sensor features.

00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:59.369
In a separate in-house extension, the context describes two changing motion conditions.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:06.288
In both cases, the goal is to interpret the response, rather than treat all variation as fault evidence.

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:10.989
The second choice is how to organize the information.

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:17.749
The core is trained to remain useful for fault recognition, including within individual sensing branches.

00:01:17.939 --> 00:01:23.613
Complementary coordinates retain information needed to reconstruct the encoded features.

00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:25.566
This gives each part a role.

00:01:25.756 --> 00:01:30.706
It also creates a way to ask whether the core remains useful as conditions change.

00:01:32.108 --> 00:01:36.236
The third choice is to test the representation through what it can do.

00:01:36.426 --> 00:01:43.062
I combine diagnostic information from one sensing branch with complementary information from another.

00:01:43.252 --> 00:01:48.766
Then I check whether the receiving representation still supports the correct fault identity.

00:01:48.956 --> 00:01:52.625
This functional test goes beyond showing a neat cluster plot.

00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:58.414
It asks whether the information remains useful after the representation is recomposed.

00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:02.581
The evaluation moves through several settings.

00:02:02.771 --> 00:02:09.244
Public waveform datasets test changes in operating conditions and previously unseen profiles.

00:02:09.434 --> 00:02:12.933
An in-house platform tests two factors changing together.

00:02:13.123 --> 00:02:16.665
Industrial wind-turbine records add a cross-device setting.

00:02:16.855 --> 00:02:24.960
Alongside classification, component studies and semantic substitutions examine what the learned core actually contributes.

00:02:26.358 --> 00:02:30.146
The results are encouraging, but they need to be read by setting.

00:02:30.336 --> 00:02:38.346
The method is competitive across the evaluated transfer tasks, with clearer advantages when operating factors change together.

00:02:38.536 --> 00:02:44.477
Recomposition preserves useful fault information in the tested continuous-profile setting.

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:48.944
Semantic interventions also show that predictions depend on the core.

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:55.022
These findings support its diagnostic role; they do not prove a unique physical representation.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:03:02.157
My contribution is to connect the central idea with a testable diagnostic system.

00:03:02.347 --> 00:03:11.935
I develop the representation design, connect learning with observed operating context, and evaluate both transfer and cross-modal recomposition.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:22.942
The broader thought is simple: a representation should be judged not only by its classification score, but also by whether the information we call shared still works when we move it.

00:03:24.358 --> 00:03:28.859
The central message is to preserve fault meaning as observations change.

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:32.932
Fault-semantic core is the term I use for that functional role.

00:03:33.122 --> 00:03:37.048
It is more precise than claiming to have found the essence of a fault.

00:03:37.238 --> 00:03:47.809
The next step is to test this idea across broader conditions, sensing combinations and deployment settings, and to understand where the organization stops being reliable.
